okay guys it's been a little while since  I've done one of these vlogs so I  thought I would update you on a few  things that are going on in my life but  before I do that there's a few things I  want to talk about few projects that I'm  not involved in but I want you to know  about first off the ever wonderful Jamie  Mills otherwise known as Jamie has it  loaded a fantastic how to study video if  you're here because of my how to study  effectively video be sure to go and  check his out cuz I'm sure he's gonna  benefit you as well  link in the description also if you'd  like to get involved in a competition  with the Ox tweet project if you don't  know what that is that's where people in  Oxford  use the hashtag ox tweet to talk about  their daily lives you can search for a  hashtag and it just gives you a snapshot  of life at the University it's a  brilliant idea and Jamie's trying to  promote it in schools across the country  and in order to do that he's sending out  postcards and he needs a design to go on  the front off those postcards and he's  looking for people to submit designs if  you'd like to get involved that  competition then there's also a link in  the description and lastly in terms of  projects if you're thinking of applying  to Durham University there is now the  Duvall project where a guy called Oscar  is vlogging about his life at the  university's archaeology and  anthropology student is the first year  if you're interested in applying to  Durham be sure to check his videos out I  really liked his first vlog so really  excited to hear more from him so what's  happening with me  I was the PhD I at the moment I'm  working towards a literature review so a  literature review is where you read all  the papers that you can are there  relevant to your particular subject so  for me that's stratosphere troposphere  coupling and you organize all that  information into a document which is  going to be about 20 pages long and you  submit that to the University the actual  goal of it isn't so much the submission  it's the process of collating the  information so I've been reading papers  left right and centre I don't even know  how many papers I've read now  fortunately stratospheric troposphere  coupling is actually a very new subject  the idea that the stratosphere influence  the troposphere the middle and lower  sections of the atmosphere has anybody  been around since the nineties really so  there aren't as many papers to go  through as there in other subjects but  all the same it's a lot of work apart  from that I've been doing a lot of  programming the attributes science which  I'm looking at the moment is looking at  pressure anomalies at set heights in the  atmosphere and in order to look at that  I have  to take in atmospheric data now to  convert it because people measure  variables now atmosphere like wind and  temperature and stuff like that that's  set pressure there are reasons for doing  that which aren't going to now but I  have to convert that to measuring those  variables at constant height which is a  relatively simple process but because  you're dealing with a large data set you  have to make sure that you're doing it  very efficiently otherwise your program  will take years to run literally I  worked out my initial program would have  taken five years to convert all the data  in addition to that stuff I'm also  taking some short courses so people who  watch my vlogs last time will know that  I took a short course in a string theory  there is a video and that come quite  seem honest I'll talk about that in a  little bit and this time I'm doing two  courses I'm doing a 20 hour course on  partial differential equations and doing  a 20 hour course on hydrodynamic  stability theory lastly to do his work  I'd like to draw your attention to a  website called Earth Nell Schoolnet this  is a website which visualizes climate  data in real time so you can look at the  wind field so the temperature feels the  pressure fields at the surface you can  look at them going up through the  atmosphere and now also ocean currents  so if you're curious about sort of ie  what the weather's doing or B how the  climate generally works go and check it  out it you can spend hours playing  around with it apart from work though as  it was last term most of what I'm doing  is singing people who watch my last term  will know that I did some a cappella C  so I'm in the madrigals group which is  early music like this  and I'm also in the Harmonix group which  is more general classical music so for  example this  and now most recently I have joined  Chapel Choir which is something that I  completely didn't expect to happen I  didn't sing at all when I was in Oxford  even though I kind of really like the  idea I just assumed I wasn't good enough  I certainly see no wasn't good enough  for the college choir so to now be  offered a place and offered offered a  scholarship in the in the chapel choir  here is absolutely unbelievable  I'm very sorry by the way if a lot of  this is old news to you if you follow me  on Twitter which is at Simon Oxford's  which you should definitely do  I hope the stuff which I'm talking about  is completely old news so sorry that I'm  boring you but so you know stick around  please of course something else which I  did in my spare time which I uploaded  quite recently was my string theory  video now you might not have seen this  but I'm really quite proud of this video  so if you missed it do you go and watch  it I want you to leave in the comments  of this video also what you liked or  didn't like about it because like I said  I want to do another video on string  theory so I want to make sure that I do  it right that string theory of video is  probably going to be 2 or 3 weeks  because I really want to do it properly  and the animation takes bloody forever  also the video got retweeted by Nicola  if you don't know who he is I'm gonna  link to his channel in the description  he's like the yoader of youtube if  you're watching this Nicola thank you so  much your praise really meant a great  deal so you're awesome  I love your videos I also recently met  with two subscribers to give an  interview about ups bridge admissions  that was by Joni and Amelia  thank you guys if you're watching it was  great to meet you both I really enjoyed  it actually  it was it was quite nice to not just  talk to a camera and talk to people who  talk back so I'm really nice to meet you  guys something I was interesting which  happened to me was I went back to Oxford  with my girlfriend to go and see a  concert in the Sheldonian theatre and  obviously was showing her around the  city and everything like she had been  and it was the first experience I had of  really going back to college and not  being there anymore and it's weird  because they made me realize how much I  missed the college even though I didn't  I didn't really think about it in those  terms at the time I really missed the  atmosphere I missed the the the the  common room and I missed all my friends  but it was really quite weird going back  and seeing locks on doors that I can go  through it makes you realize that you  don't appreciate stuff truly until it's  gone so with that in mind a massive  congratulations to everybody who's got  an offer and so hasn't been to Oxford  yet but knows they are going to also a  big shout out that the guy who in some  Peters bar stopped me to say thank you  for making my videos I didn't catch your  name so if you're watching this that  really meant a huge deal to me like  that's never happened to me before  people have just outright stopped me and  said thank you actually speaking of  Oxford I'm going to be going back in a  few weeks time for a talk the physics  society is doing an evening of student  talks and have invited me to give the  one which closes everything off so if  you're in Oxford keep an eye out for  that on the physics Society Facebook and  I might see there that's pretty much  everything it's been a little while like  I say since I've done release there's a  lot to talk about but I think that's  that's all the important stuff I II was  thinking that I might close out this  video with a little clip from Chapel  Choir because we sang a piece called  North dimittis by Gustav Holst and it  might be the most beautiful piece I've  ever sung and I'm I think the choir  really did it justice I'm really quite  tempted to upload it in full in its own  separate video on this channel so give  it a listen and if you like what you  hear you want to hear more then let me  know and I will see you quite soon  thanks for watching

